Each of these circumstances poses a different obstacle for the discipline of continuous dealings in securing self-governance.
To work well, and some would argue to work at all, in addition to requiring repeated interaction, the discipline of continuous dealings requires small and socially homogeneous populations, populations whose members don't confront the prospect of violence, and populations whose members don't discount the future too heavily.
The essays in Parts I–III analyze cases of successful self-governance despite deviations from these conditions, and thus mechanisms of self-governance that go beyond the discipline of continuous dealings alone.